+## Model fallback
+
+To keep a run going through a rate limit, a billing issue, or bad credentials
+on one provider, point a model at one or more others to fall back to. Add
+`fallback_models` alongside a model's `name`, `use`, and `model` fields:
+
+```yaml
+models:
+  - name: claude-sonnet-4
+    use: langchain_anthropic:ChatAnthropic
+    model: claude-sonnet-4-20250514
+    api_key: $ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
+    fallback_models: [gpt-4]   # tried in order if claude-sonnet-4 keeps failing
+
+  - name: gpt-4
+    use: langchain_openai:ChatOpenAI
+    model: gpt-4
+    api_key: $OPENAI_API_KEY
+```
+
+Each name in `fallback_models` must be another entry in this `models:` list,
+with its own working credentials — DeerFlow does not share or translate
+credentials between providers. Fallback triggers when a request to the
+primary model either exhausts its own retry/backoff budget (a sustained rate
+limit, a busy provider, or a burst-rate throttle) or is rejected outright for
+a quota/billing or authentication reason — exactly the cases a different
+provider account can plausibly route around. It does **not** trigger for an
+unclassified error, since swapping providers wouldn't fix a real bug and
+could mask it. Each fallback gets the same retry/backoff policy as the
+primary before DeerFlow moves to the next one in the list; only once every
+fallback has also failed does the run see the final failure message.
+
+A name that doesn't resolve to a configured model, repeats an earlier entry,
+or points back at the model itself is dropped with a warning at agent-build
+time rather than failing startup. Off unless configured, so existing
+deployments are unaffected. This is a same-request fallback for
+availability, not a cost-based router: it does not consider per-model
+`pricing`, and a fallback model does not inherit the primary's sampling
+settings (`temperature`, `max_tokens`, thinking configuration, etc.) — each
+model's own configured settings apply as-is when it is used.
+
 ## Config Versioning
 
 `config.example.yaml` contains a `config_version` field that tracks schema changes. When the example version is higher than your local `config.yaml`, the application emits a startup warning:
